Chapter 1 – The Civilizational Problem

Human civilization has always operated under constraints of communication delay, resource scarcity, incomplete information, and biological fragility. Interplanetary expansion magnifies these constraints to a qualitatively new regime. The one-way light-time between Earth and Mars varies between 4 and 24 minutes; for the outer planets it reaches hours, and for interstellar distances it stretches to years. Real-time bilateral control, the bedrock of all terrestrial institutions, becomes physically impossible. Any governance architecture that presupposes instantaneous centralized command is therefore infeasible.

Colonies separated by such delays must possess local autonomy for survival yet remain participants in a larger cooperative system. The central question is thus:

What mathematical architecture allows autonomous civilizations to remain coherent despite spatial separation, communication latency, uncertainty, and continuous evolution?

Current space research focuses overwhelmingly on physical infrastructure—propulsion, habitats, life support. The organizational layer, which determines whether that infrastructure is used sustainably, lacks an equivalent first-principles treatment. This dissertation provides exactly that, building on the deep traditions of cybernetic viable systems, polycentric governance, delay-tolerant networking, and distributed optimization, and extending them into a unified mathematical framework for multiplanetary existence.

