Chapter 2 – First Principles and Related Work

2.1 Immutable First Principles

Every civilization is constrained by immutable laws. We refine five principles with quantitative backing.

Principle 1 (Physics – Finite Signal Speed). No information or control signal can travel faster than c. The interplanetary round-trip time \tau_{\text{RTT}} ranges from 7–44 minutes for Mars to years for interstellar distances, ruling out any protocol that requires a continuous handshake. Admissible control and coordination algorithms are thus restricted to the class of fully decentralized methods; no fusion center can exist.

Principle 2 (Information – Incomplete Knowledge). Every decision-making unit acts on a local information set \mathcal{I}_i(t) that is a strict subset of the global state, received through rate-limited, delayed channels.

Principle 3 (Control – Viability and Feedback). Long-term stability requires feedback that keeps the system within a viability kernel. Formally, a dynamical system \dot{x} = f(x,u,\omega) remains sustainable if x(t) \in K for all t, where K is the set of acceptable states (oxygen, energy, social stability). The viability kernel \text{Viab}(K) is the set of initial states from which there exists a control policy u(\cdot) that maintains x(t) \in K indefinitely (Aubin, 1991) [10].

Principle 4 (Evolution – Continuous Adaptation). Both system parameters and the environment are non-stationary. Adaptive mechanisms must therefore operate at all timescales, from seconds to centuries.

Principle 5 (Energy – Thermodynamic Cost of Organization). Every information processing and decision-making step dissipates at least k_B T \ln 2 per bit erased (Landauer, 1961). Hierarchical, modular architectures minimize energy dissipation because a flat architecture forces every node to erase the irrelevant correlations contained in raw global data, incurring a bit-erasure cost proportional to the mismatch between the node's internal model and the full complexity of the environment. Recent results in energy-aware distributed optimization demonstrate that structured hierarchical aggregation can reduce communication energy by up to 80% [2307.04913]. Thus, the civilizational architecture itself is subject to thermodynamic selection.

2.2 The Viable System Model as Antecedent

The idea that a viable system must contain five recursively nested subsystems (operations, coordination, control, intelligence, and policy) was formalized by Stafford Beer’s Viable System Model (VSM, 1972). VSM is built on Ashby’s Law of Requisite Variety and applies identical cybernetic descriptions to every recursive level.

FCA inherits from VSM the necessity of recursive decomposition, local autonomy within global cohesion, and the variety-attenuation requirement. However, FCA extends VSM in five critical respects:

1. Explicit interplanetary delay constraints. VSM was designed for Earth-bound latencies. FCA reformulates variety attenuation as constrained optimization over a time-varying delay graph derived from orbital mechanics.
2. A mathematical coherence measure. VSM asserts that recursion preserves viability; FCA defines fractal coherence operationally as a bounded residual after delay-aware distributed optimization, monitored via scaling exponents.
3. Thermodynamic grounding. VSM is cybernetic but not energetic. FCA embeds information processing in Landauer’s limit, making hierarchy depth a consequence of minimizing dissipated energy per viable decision.
4. Scalable collective memory with phase transition. VSM does not model storage and verification of civilizational knowledge across disconnected periods. FCA’s memory tuple and critical density theorem provide this.
5. Integration with contemporary distributed optimization and DTN. VSM predates both; FCA operationalizes viability as the convergence neighborhood of a delay-aware consensus algorithm.

Thus, VSM is the qualitative grandfather; FCA provides the mathematical apparatus that makes the theory falsifiable and computationally implementable for multiplanetary scales.

2.3 Other Foundational Literature

· Fractal and Hierarchical Network Theory: Song et al. [cond-mat/0505189] established box-covering methods to measure fractal dimension in complex networks. Scaling theory [2306.13751] introduces microscopic and macroscopic exponents. Self-organized networks exhibit universal fractal scaling regardless of size [6], implying a cost to maintaining fractal links that must appear in the civilizational objective function.
· Polycentric Governance: Ostrom’s (1990) design principles for common-pool resource management have been formalized for multi-agent systems [20]; polycentric governance distributes authority across multiple overlapping centres that coordinate through shared rules and negotiation [18,19].
· Interplanetary DTN and Goal-Oriented Communications: The Bundle Protocol (RFC 5050; CCSDS) provides store-and-forward custody transfer for deep-space links. Goal-oriented flow control [2409.14534] minimizes Age-of-Information by transmitting only survival-critical data when timely.
· Hierarchical Federated Learning: FedHC [2502.12783] achieves 3× reduction in processing time and 2× reduction in energy through clustered aggregation; HALoS [2506.04531] provides hierarchical asynchronous local SGD over slow networks.

