Chapter 5 – Distributed Optimization of Civilizational Coherence

This chapter constitutes the mathematical core of FCA. We reformulate the civilization’s coordination as a delay-aware, hierarchical consensus optimization problem under viability constraints.

5.1 The Civilizational Graph with Time-Varying Delay

Let \mathcal{C} = (V, E, \mathcal{T}), where V is the set of autonomous nodes, E \subseteq V \times V are communication/dependency edges, and \mathcal{T}: E \times \mathbb{R}_+ \to \mathbb{R}_+ is the time-varying round-trip latency function derived from orbital mechanics. The graph is a dynamic contact graph; edges appear and disappear according to predictable contact windows. Over any time window of length T_{\text{max}} (e.g., one synodic period), the union graph is assumed connected.

5.2 Local Objective with Coherence Penalty

Each node i maintains a local state vector x_i \in \mathbb{R}^{d_i} (resources, policy parameters, belief estimates). The local objective to be minimized is:

J_i(x_i) = \alpha_i \ell_i(x_i) + \beta_i \text{Energy}_i(x_i) + \gamma_i \sum_{j \in \mathcal{N}(i)} \|x_i - z_{ij}\|^2 + \delta_i \|x_i - \Pi_i(\bar{x})\|^2,

where:

· \ell_i: local mission loss (may be convex or nonconvex),
· \text{Energy}_i: computation and communication dissipation (bounded below by Landauer’s limit),
· The third term is a disagreement cost with neighbors j, using auxiliary consensus variables z_{ij}; this penalizes deviation from directly connected nodes and enforces alignment within a holon.
· The fourth term is the coherence residual: \Pi_i is a known, scale-specific projection from the global latent vector \bar{x}. The weight \delta_i controls tightness of coupling to civilizational invariants; it is subject to a lower bound imposed by the viability kernel—a node that persistently ignores coherence risks exclusion from shared resources.

The parameters (\alpha_i, \beta_i, \gamma_i, \delta_i) are allowed to vary by node type and scale, reflecting the different trade-offs between local autonomy and global alignment.

5.3 Global Problem as Hierarchical Federated Optimization

The civilization’s coordination problem is

\min_{\{x_i\}} \sum_i J_i(x_i) \quad \text{subject to} \quad x_i = z_{ij} = x_j \;\; \forall (i,j) \in E,\; x_i \in \text{Viab}(K_i),

with the additional requirement that \bar{x} is itself updated on a slow timescale.

We solve this via a two-timescale hierarchical federated approach:

· Inner loop (fast): Within each holon, nodes run a delay-compensated Consensus ADMM (C-ADMM) over the DTN contact graph. They exchange bundles containing z_{ij} and dual variables, using the known future contact schedule. Convergence is to a local consensus state consistent with the current \bar{x}.
· Outer loop (slow): Holon aggregators (regional parameter servers) collect statistical summaries of the converged x_i and propose updates to \bar{x} using a Byzantine-tolerant federated averaging protocol, transmitted over interplanetary DTN links. The outer loop runs on constitutional timescales (months to years), minimizing the sum of coherence residuals across all nodes. This ensures \bar{x} evolves as the minimal-distortion shared representation of civilization’s functional invariants.

This nested structure directly mirrors the FedHC [2502.12783] and HALoS [2506.04531] architectures, which have demonstrated 2–3× reductions in energy and communication overhead.

5.4 Convergence Under Unbounded Predictable Delay

Existing asynchronous ADMM convergence proofs assume bounded delays. Interplanetary delays can be arbitrarily long but are precisely predictable from ephemerides. We exploit this predictability.

Theorem 2 (Distributed Coherence Convergence).
Assume each J_i is \mu-strongly convex and L-smooth, the contact graph over any window of length T_{\text{max}} is connected, and the round-trip delays \mathcal{T}(e,t) are known in advance for a horizon H covering the maximum light-time. Then there exists a delay-compensated C-ADMM algorithm that converges linearly to a neighborhood of the global optimum, with radius

R = \kappa \cdot \max_{e,t} \mathcal{T}(e,t) \cdot \frac{L}{\mu} \cdot \frac{1}{\gamma_{\min}},

where \kappa depends on graph topology and \gamma_{\min} is the minimum consensus penalty weight among active edges.

Significance. This theorem provides an explicit trade-off between interplanetary distance, convergence speed, and required coupling strength. If \max \mathcal{T} grows too large for a given \gamma_{\min}, the residual R exceeds the viability-allowed coherence threshold \epsilon_{\text{crit}}, and the system is predicted to fragment. This is the operational falsification condition for the Fractal Civilizational Principle.

5.5 Coherence Monitoring

Fractal coherence can be monitored continuously by tracking:

· The time-averaged per-node residual \epsilon_i.
· The fractal dimension d_B from box-covering.
· The consensus convergence rate under varying delay: a persistent increase in residual after communication blackouts without recovery indicates drift toward fragmentation.

The AI Insight Layer (Chapter 7) is responsible for computing these metrics and issuing alerts.

