Chapter 6 – Communication Substrate: DTN and Goal-Oriented Age-of-Information

Interplanetary communication cannot rely on TCP/IP. The Bundle Protocol (BP) of Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networking [RFC 5050, CCSDS] stores and forwards entire bundles, carrying all necessary metadata for custody transfer. We model the communication cost in the optimization explicitly via DTN semantics.

Definition (Age-of-Information for DTN). For a data item generated at t_{\text{gen}} and delivered at t_{\text{del}}, the Age-of-Information (AoI) at the receiver is \Delta(t) = t - t_{\text{gen}}. In a multi-hop DTN, the expected AoI is a function of bundle custody time, contact window uncertainty, and queueing delays.

The communication penalty for node i becomes:

R_i = \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{\text{bundles}} \phi(\Delta_{\text{arrival}}) \right] + \text{Energy}_{\text{TX}}.

Goal-oriented communications [2409.14534] redesigns flow control so that only information necessary for maintaining viability and coherence is transmitted, and only when timely. The coherence term C_i in the objective is then directly the reduction in critical AoI: a node’s actions that lower the AoI of survival-layer parameters for others increase its coherence contribution. The ISS DTN emulator [2605.21624] provides an open-source testbed that we propose to adapt for FCA simulation.

