Chapter 10 – Limitations and Future Work

This theoretical architecture does not yet incorporate:

1. Game-theoretic defection. Nodes may act strategically (free-riding, resource hoarding). Future work should integrate mechanism design and the Tragedy of the Commons into the local objectives.
2. Relativistic effects. For interstellar scales, proper time differences and relativistic signal propagation alter the communication model.
3. Non-uniform energy availability. Solar irradiance at Saturn is ~1.2% of Earth’s; nodes in energy-poor environments will have tighter energy budgets, affecting their ability to participate in consensus steps.
4. Secession and fragmentation dynamics. The condition under which a holon rationally exits the global coherence agreement (secession as viability-kernel exit) warrants a dedicated game-theoretic treatment.
5. Empirical validation. The simulation framework is proposed but not executed; real-world implementation on the ISS DTN testbed remains a future milestone.

These limitations define a clear research program for the coming decade. The mathematical framework presented here is designed to accommodate these extensions without foundational revision.

