Appendix A – Fractal Coherence Condition: Box-Covering and Renormalization Group Invariance

A.1 Definitions

Let \mathcal{C}=(V,E) be the civilization graph where nodes are decision-making units. The information distance d_{ij} between nodes i and j is the minimum achievable Age‑of‑Information over the DTN contact plan; it satisfies the metric axioms. For a given box size \ell_B>0, a box is a subset of nodes with diameter at most \ell_B. The box‑covering method [Song et al. 2005] finds the minimum number N_B(\ell_B) of boxes needed to cover all nodes. If

N_B(\ell_B) \sim \ell_B^{-d_B}

over a range [\ell_{\min},\ell_{\max}] with \ell_{\max}/\ell_{\min}\gg1, the network is fractal with dimension d_B.

A.2 Renormalization group (RG) transformation

Define the RG map \mathcal{R} as follows. Given a box cover at scale \ell_B, each box becomes a super‑node. Two super‑nodes are connected by a directed edge if there exists at least one edge between their constituent nodes. The weight w_{kl} of the super‑edge is the total effective interaction strength \sum_{i\in\text{box}_k,\,j\in\text{box}_l} w_{ij} normalized to preserve the total strength. This process yields a new graph \mathcal{C}'=\mathcal{R}(\mathcal{C}).

A.3 Statistical invariance

Let P(x) be the probability density of a structural or functional metric x (degree, betweenness, local information flow, failure recovery time). The civilization is fractally coherent if for any such metric the distribution after one RG step satisfies

P_{\mathcal{C}'}(x) = P_{\mathcal{C}}(x) \qquad \text{(in distribution)}.

Equivalently, all moments \langle x^k\rangle are unchanged up to a trivial scale factor absorbed in the definition of the metric.

Proof sketch of invariance under hierarchical self‑similarity.
Assume the network is generated by a branching process where each node at level h splits into b children, and connections are formed preferentially within the same branch. Song et al. [2005] showed that such hierarchical networks display fractal scaling and that the renormalized network is statistically identical to the original if the connection probability satisfies a power‑law relation. In the FCA context, the same structural self‑similarity is posited as an architectural principle; therefore, by construction, the RG flow leaves the distribution invariant. A rigorous proof proceeds by induction on the hierarchy depth, using the multiplicative property of edge probabilities under coarse‑graining. ∎

A.4 Invariance of decision rules

A local decision rule is a mapping \pi^{(s)}:\mathcal{X}^{(s)}\to\mathcal{U}^{(s)} from the local state space to actions. Let \Pi^{(s)}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{X}^{(s)} be a projection that extracts the scale‑s components of the full state. Scale invariance of the decision rule means there exists a function g independent of s such that

\pi^{(s)}(x^{(s)}) = g\bigl(\Pi^{(s)}(x)\bigr).

This condition can be depicted as a commuting diagram: coarse‑graining the state and then applying g yields the same action as applying the fine‑grained rule and then projecting. The existence of such a g is assumed in the definition of fractal coherence; its necessity is shown by requiring that the policy updates in the hierarchical optimization (Appendix E) remain consistent across scales.

