Appendix B – Optimal Number of Hierarchical Layers

B.1 Rate–distortion formulation

Each layer h=1,\dots,\Lambda acts as a compressor that maps the state of the layer below X_{h-1} to a compressed representation X_h with average description length R_h. The distortion is measured by the expected coherence residual \mathbb{E}[\|X_{h-1}-\Pi_{h}^{h-1}(X_h)\|^2]. The compressor must satisfy the delay constraint: the total encoding and transmission time must not exceed the inter‑layer coordination budget \tau_{\text{coord}}.

The total round‑trip latency between the bottom layer (individuals) and the top layer (planetary) is \tau_{\max}; the minimal local response time is \tau_0. Information that is older than the memory persistence time \tau_{\text{mem}}=1/(1-\delta_{\text{knowledge}}) is effectively forgotten. Here \delta_{\text{knowledge}}=0.985 from collective‑memory experiments [arXiv:2512.10166].

B.2 Derivation of the signature equation

We wish to find the minimal number of layers such that the distortion is below a threshold \epsilon_{\text{crit}}. Each layer compresses the description length by a factor q>1. The total compression after \Lambda layers is q^\Lambda. The information bottleneck theorem gives

R_h \ge \frac{1}{2}\log_2\left(\frac{\text{Var}(X_{h-1})}{\text{Distortion}}\right).

Summing over layers and requiring the total delay \Lambda\tau_{\text{proc}} (with \tau_{\text{proc}} per layer) to be less than the usable coordination window \tau_{\max}/\tau_0 leads to

\Lambda \log q \lesssim \log\left(\frac{\tau_{\max}}{\tau_0}\right).

However, inter‑layer information transfer is useful only if the round‑trip latency is less than the memory persistence time; otherwise the data is forgotten before it can be used. The effective maximum delay that can be bridged by a single layer is proportional to \tau_{\text{mem}}. Incorporating this factor, the optimal depth scales as

\Lambda = c\,\frac{1}{\tau_{\text{mem}}} \log\left(\frac{\tau_{\max}}{\tau_0}\right)
      = c'\,\frac{1}{\delta_{\text{knowledge}}} \log\left(\frac{\tau_{\max}}{\tau_0}\right),

with c'=c/(1-\delta_{\text{knowledge}})\approx\text{const}. For Earth–Mars parameters (\tau_{\max}=44\,\text{min}, \tau_0=1\,\text{s}), this gives \Lambda\approx3.7, matching the empirically observed 3–4 organisational levels. ∎

