Appendix C – Thermodynamic Selection of Hierarchical Modularity

C.1 Landauer erasure cost in coordination networks

Consider a node i that, in a flat fully‑connected architecture, receives the raw global state X_{\text{global}} of size d. Its internal model needs to erase the irrelevant mutual information I(X_{\text{global}};Y_i) before making a local decision u_i, where Y_i is the local relevant variable. The minimum dissipated energy per decision is k_B T \ln 2 \cdot I(X_{\text{global}};Y_i) (Landauer 1961). Using the maximum‑entropy Gaussian bound, I(X_{\text{global}};Y_i) \le \frac{1}{2}\log\left(1+\frac{\text{Var}(X_{\text{global}})}{\text{Var}(Y_i)}\right) \propto \log N for a system with N independent components, or can scale as N if the global state is fully entangled. In the worst case of strong coupling, I \sim \mathcal{O}(N).

Thus the total energy dissipation per global update is \Omega(N^2) in a flat network of N nodes.

C.2 Hierarchical compression reduces erasure cost

In a fractal hierarchy with span of control k (constant), each node only receives the state of its k children and its parent. The relevant mutual information is bounded by a constant independent of N. The total communication and erasure cost across all nodes scales as \mathcal{O}(N \log_k N) = \mathcal{O}(N \log N). For large N, the flat architecture would require an energy budget exceeding any physical limit, whereas the fractal architecture remains feasible. Hence thermodynamic selection favors hierarchical modularity.

