Appendix D – Flat Governance Impossibility Theorem

D.1 Formalisation

Let N autonomous nodes need to maintain a common invariant \bar{x} against Byzantine faults. In a flat governance structure, every node must verify every other node’s compliance. The number of verification links is \binom{N}{2}=\mathcal{O}(N^2). If each verification consumes a fixed amount of attention or energy e, the total cost is \mathcal{O}(eN^2).

D.2 Critical size from energy bound

From the viability constraint, the total energy available for governance per unit time is E_{\text{tot}}. The maximum number of nodes that can be sustained in a flat network is N_{\text{crit}} \approx \sqrt{2E_{\text{tot}}/e}. Beyond this size, the system cannot maintain the verification density required for rule of law; coherence degrades inevitably, as proven by [arXiv:2601.14325] for two‑tier structures.

D.3 Fractal architecture scaling

In a polycentric holarchy with L layers and span k, each node only monitors its k subordinates and k peers, and is monitored by one superior. The total verification count is \mathcal{O}(N k). Hence the cost scales linearly, and large N is possible without exceeding the energy budget. ∎

