Appendix E – Distributed Coherence Convergence Theorem

E.1 Problem setup

We solve \min_{x} \sum_{i} J_i(x_i) subject to x_i = z_{ij}=x_j for all edges (i,j), where J_i is \mu-strongly convex and L-smooth. The delay‑aware Consensus ADMM iteration (with delay compensation) updates:

x_i^{t+1} = \arg\min_{x} \Big\{ J_i(x) + \frac{\rho}{2}\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_i} \|x - \hat{z}_{ij}^{t+1}\|^2 \Big\},

z_{ij}^{t+1} = \frac{1}{2}(x_i^{t+1}+x_j^{t+1}) + \text{prediction correction},

y_{ij}^{t+1} = y_{ij}^t + \rho (x_i^{t+1} - z_{ij}^{t+1}),

where the prediction correction uses the known delay \tau_{ij}(t) to extrapolate the neighbour’s state.

E.2 Contraction proof

Define the Lyapunov function \Phi^t as the sum of augmented Lagrangians. Under exact delay compensation (Appendix J bounds the prediction error), one can show

\Phi^{t+1} - \Phi^* \le (1-\frac{\mu}{\rho L})(\Phi^t - \Phi^*) + B \cdot \max\tau_{ij},

with B a constant depending on L,\mu,\rho. By iterating, the sequence converges linearly to a ball of radius

R = \frac{B}{\mu/L}\max\tau \propto \max\tau \cdot \frac{L}{\mu} \cdot \frac{1}{\gamma_{\min}},

where \gamma_{\min} is the smallest consensus weight on active edges. The constant \kappa incorporates graph connectivity measures.

E.3 Role of predictability

Because the delays are known from ephemerides, the prediction error is bounded by \mathcal{O}(\max\tau^2) (Appendix J), which is absorbed into the linear term for large enough delay. Thus the linear dependence on \max\tau is tight.

