Appendix F – Critical Density Theorem for Collective Memory

F.1 Model description

Agents are placed on a spatial grid. Each agent i has personal memory M_i and can deposit environmental traces T(x,y) with persistence \delta_k for knowledge and \delta_d for danger. Agents interact at rate \rho (verification events). The order parameter is the overlap m = \frac{1}{N}\sum_i \langle \text{sign}(M_i \cdot \bar{x}) \rangle.

F.2 Mean‑field analysis

In mean field, the memory update is M_i^{t+1} = \tanh(\beta ( \sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_i} J_{ij} M_j^t + h T_i^t )). The environment trace evolves as T^{t+1} = \delta T^t + \alpha m^t. Stationary solution yields the self‑consistency equation

m = \tanh(\beta ( \rho J_0 m + \frac{\alpha h}{1-\delta} m )).

A non‑zero solution exists only if the effective coupling J_{\text{eff}} = \rho J_0 + \frac{\alpha h}{1-\delta} exceeds the critical value 1/\beta. Solving for \rho gives

\rho_c = \frac{1}{J_0}\left( \frac{1}{\beta} - \frac{\alpha h}{1-\delta} \right).

With the parameters from the emergent collective memory study [arXiv:2512.10166] (\delta=0.985, \alpha,h calibrated), \rho_c = 0.230 \pm 0.030. Below this density, only the trivial solution m=0 is stable, meaning collective memory fragments. Above it, the system spontaneously sustains a shared memory. ∎

