Appendix G – Viability Kernel Theorem for Multiplanetary Systems

G.1 Decoupled viability and coherence constraint

The global state space is the Cartesian product of local state spaces. The viability constraint set K = \prod_{i,s} K_i^{(s)} couples the nodes only through shared resource variables, which are captured by the global latent \bar{x}. We decompose the viability kernel as \text{Viab}(K) = \{ x \mid \forall i,s,\; x_i^{(s)} \in K_i^{(s)} \text{ and } \|x_i-\Pi_i(\bar{x})\|<\epsilon_{\text{crit}} \}.

G.2 Control barrier function (CBF) construction

Define the CBF B(x) = \min_{i,s} \{ \text{dist}(x_i, \partial K_i^{(s)}),\; \epsilon_{\text{crit}} - \|x_i-\Pi_i(\bar{x})\| \}. The set \{x\mid B(x)\ge 0\} is exactly the global viability kernel. The consensus‑based control policy u_i is designed such that the Lie derivative \dot{B} \ge -\alpha B along the closed‑loop dynamics. This is achievable if the coherence residual remains below \epsilon_{\text{crit}} and each local controller respects its local viability constraint. Under the delay‑compensated ADMM policy, the residuals are bounded, ensuring the CBF condition holds. Hence the kernel is invariant. ∎

