Appendix H – Fractal Civilizational Coherence Theorem (Necessary Condition)

H.1 Statement

For a multiplanetary civilization to remain within the viability kernel indefinitely with probability >1-\epsilon, it must satisfy: (1) fractal coherence, (2) verification density \rho>\rho_c, (3) \Lambda \ge c\,\frac{1}{\delta_{\text{knowledge}}}\log(\tau_{\max}/\tau_0), (4) delay‑compensated DTN‑based consensus.

H.2 Proof by contradiction

Assume (1) fails. Then the coherence residual \max_i \epsilon_i \ge \epsilon_{\text{crit}}. By the CBF condition (Appendix G), the set where B(x)\ge 0 is not forward invariant; exit from the viability kernel occurs with positive probability in finite time.

Assume (2) fails (\rho\le\rho_c). Then by the Critical Density Theorem (Appendix F), collective memory decays to zero. Nodes lose the shared \bar{x}, causing \epsilon_i to diverge, again violating the CBF condition.

Assume (3) or (4) fails. Then the convergence radius R from Appendix E exceeds \epsilon_{\text{crit}} (or the communication protocol cannot maintain the consensus). The system cannot keep the residuals within the viable band, leading to fragmentation.

Thus all four conditions are necessary for indefinite viability. ∎

