Appendix I – Coherence Residual and Falsifiability Bound

I.1 Derivation of \epsilon_{\text{crit}}

Let the shared invariant space be \mathbb{R}^m. The viability boundary is defined by a set of inequalities g_k(\bar{x})\le 0. For a node with projection \Pi_i, the local constraint becomes g_k(\Pi_i(x_i)+\delta)\le 0, where \delta is the discrepancy. By Taylor expansion, the safety margin is d_{\min} = \min_k |g_k(\bar{x})|/\|\nabla g_k\|. If the residual exceeds this margin, the local state violates at least one constraint. Hence we set

\epsilon_{\text{crit}} = \sigma_{\min}(\Pi_i)^{-1} \cdot d_{\min},

where \sigma_{\min} is the smallest singular value of the projection (assumed non‑degenerate). In practice, for linearised constraints, \epsilon_{\text{crit}} is the maximum permissible deviation from the shared invariant that still keeps all local systems within their safety margins. This value can be computed from the engineering specifications of life‑support margins, energy budgets, etc.

I.2 Falsifiability

The theory predicts that if measured \max_i \epsilon_i exceeds \epsilon_{\text{crit}} for a sustained period, the civilization will exit the viability kernel. Conversely, if it survives, the measured residual must be below the threshold. This provides a direct empirical test.

