Appendix J – Delay‑Predictability Exploitation Lemma

J.1 Prediction error bound

Consider two nodes i and j with one‑way delay \tau. Node i receives a measurement of x_j(t-\tau). To predict x_j(t), node i integrates the known dynamics \dot{x}_j = f(x_j,u_j) from the delayed state using the stored control history u_j(s) for s\in[t-\tau,t]. The true state satisfies

x_j(t) = x_j(t-\tau) + \int_{t-\tau}^t f(x_j(s),u_j(s)) ds.

The predicted state is \hat{x}_j(t) = x_j(t-\tau) + \int_{t-\tau}^t f(\hat{x}_j(s),u_j(s)) ds with zero initial error. By the Lipschitz property \|f(x,u)-f(y,u)\|\le L_f\|x-y\|, Gronwall’s inequality gives

\|\hat{x}_j(t)-x_j(t)\| \le L_f \int_{t-\tau}^t \|\hat{x}_j(s)-x_j(s)\| ds.

Thus the error is bounded by

\|\hat{x}_j(t)-x_j(t)\| \le \frac{L_f}{2} \tau^2 \sup\|u\|.

J.2 Impact on ADMM

This prediction error adds an additive perturbation to the consensus variable z_{ij}. In the Lyapunov analysis of Appendix E, this term contributes a bounded drift proportional to \tau^2; however, the linear convergence rate ensures that the cumulative effect yields a residual ball radius linear in \max\tau. Hence the delayed system still converges to a bounded neighbourhood whose size scales with the maximum delay, as stated in Theorem 2. ∎

